Apple last week <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/08/apple-unveils-a-more-powerful-mac-mini-featuring-the-all-new-m6-and-m5-pro/">announced</a> two new <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/mac-mini/">Mac mini</a> models, one with the all-new <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/25/apple-reveals-m6/">M6 chip</a> and one with the M5 Pro chip that debuted in the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> earlier this year.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/08/mac-mini-2026-blue.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1061225" /><br/>
Somewhat like the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; last year, Apple has again placed its newest silicon in the entry-level machine and a previous-generation Pro chip in the high-end machine, so the more expensive &zwnj;Mac mini&zwnj; is not simply a faster version of the cheaper one. The M6 model starts at &#36;899 with a 12-core CPU, 16GB of unified memory, and 256GB of storage. The M5 Pro model starts at &#36;1,699 with a 15-core CPU, 24GB of memory, and 512GB of storage, and it can be configured with up to an 18-core CPU and a 20-core GPU.<br/>
<br/>
Both are the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/25/mac-studio-mac-mini-tidbits/">highest starting prices</a> Apple has ever set for the &zwnj;Mac mini&zwnj;, which makes the &#36;800 gap between the two models more consequential than in previous generations. Our guide helps to answer the question of how to decide which of these two &zwnj;Mac mini&zwnj; models is best for you.<br/>
<br/>
Both models are <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/25/apples-mac-mini-mac-studio-available-pre-order/">available to pre-order</a> now and launch on Tuesday, September 22. The two machines are physically identical and share the majority of their hardware. The shared features include:<br/>
 <br/>
<ul><br/>
<li>The same 5.0 by 5.0 inch aluminum enclosure in silver, unchanged since 2024</li><br/>
<li>Neural Accelerators in every GPU core, a first for the &zwnj;Mac mini&zwnj;</li><br/>
<li>Wi-Fi 7 and Bluetooth 6 via the N1 chip, another first for the &zwnj;Mac mini&zwnj; </li><br/>
<li>2.5Gb Ethernet as standard, with a 10Gb Ethernet configuration option</li><br/>
<li>Two front USB-C ports and a headphone jack with high-impedance headphone support</li><br/>
<li>An HDMI port and native support for up to three external displays</li><br/>
<li>Genlock over USB-C for synchronizing a display with a camera such as the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17-pro/">iPhone 17 Pro</a></li><br/>
<li>macOS 27 with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-intelligence/">Apple Intelligence</a> and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> AI</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
